@prompt
Probes inside function definitions collect one sample *per call*. The function defined below, `watering_amount`, calculates the amount of water a plant should receive. This function is called three times at the bottom of the program. By default probes show you values from one call at a time, but you can also opt to see more.

# Tasks
1. Probe the case expression: caret on the `case` token, **Cmd/Ctrl+E**. Notice the small count badge on the probe: it shows **3**, the number of samples this probe collected (one per call).
2. The sample reads `220`, with arrows beside it. Step through the three calls with the arrows, or click the sample and use **←/→**.
3. Press **Space** for *Many* mode to see all three at once; **Space** again to go back. Alternatively, click the **Samples** toggle in the sidebar. (A long value gets more room in one-at-a-time mode, so switching back is a quick way to see detail.)
4. Finish the third call at the bottom (`watering_amount(50, Waning)`) so all three run. Type `Waning` slowly and watch the sample. The partial constructor is stuck in the case until completed.

@hint
Finish the third call: `watering_amount(50, Waning)`. Then probe the `case` and use ←/→.

@code
type MoonPhase = New + Waxing + Full + Waning in
let watering_amount(base: Int, phase: MoonPhase): Int =
  case phase
  | New => base + 50
  | Full => base - 30
  | _ => base
  end
in
watering_amount(250, Full);
watering_amount(180, New);
watering_amount(50, ¿)

@test
test answer == 50 end
